Building this package on Debian 13
==================================

The toolchain
-------------

porthole's MSRV is 1.87. Debian 13 (trixie) ships rustc 1.85.0:

    # apt-cache policy rustc
    rustc:
      Installed: (none)
      Candidate: 1.85.0+dfsg3-1
      Version table:
         1.94.1+dfsg1-1~bpo13+3 100
            100 http://deb.debian.org/debian trixie-backports/main amd64 Packages
         1.85.0+dfsg3-1 500
            500 http://deb.debian.org/debian trixie/main amd64 Packages

    # apt-cache policy cargo
    cargo:
      Installed: (none)
      Candidate: 1.85.0+dfsg3-1
      Version table:
         1.94.1+dfsg1-1~bpo13+3 100
            100 http://deb.debian.org/debian trixie-backports/main amd64 Packages
         1.85.0+dfsg3-1 500
            500 http://deb.debian.org/debian trixie/main amd64 Packages

(Run in a debian:13 container against deb.debian.org on 2026-09-07.)

trixie-backports carries 1.94.1, which is why debian/control says
`rustc (>= 1.87)` and `cargo (>= 1.87)` rather than fetching a toolchain
during the build. The dependency is a real Debian dependency on a real
Debian package, so an unprepared machine is told exactly what is wrong:

    $ apt-get build-dep .
     builddeps:. : Depends: cargo (>= 1.87) but 1.85.0+dfsg3-1 is to be installed
                   Depends: rustc (>= 1.87) but 1.85.0+dfsg3-1 is to be installed
    E: Unable to correct problems, you have held broken packages.

rather than failing halfway through the build with a compiler error nobody
would read as a version problem.

Enabling the suite is not enough on its own -- the output above is from a
container that has it enabled. Backports sit at pin priority 100, and apt
does not reach past the candidate to satisfy a versioned dependency. Two
things do work, both run in a debian:13 container:

    # Ask for the whole build-dep resolution against backports.
    apt-get build-dep -t trixie-backports .
    # -> rustc, cargo 1.94.1+dfsg1-1~bpo13+3; also debhelper 13.31~bpo13+1,
    #    because -t raises the suite for everything, not just the two.

    # Or pin the toolchain alone and leave the rest on trixie. rustc's own
    # Depends: libstd-rust-dev (= <same version>) is why the stanza cannot
    # name just the two: pinning `rustc cargo` alone leaves apt with
    # "rustc : Depends: libstd-rust-dev (= 1.94.1+dfsg1-1~bpo13+3) but
    # 1.85.0+dfsg3-1 is to be installed".
    cat > /etc/apt/preferences.d/rust-backports <<'PIN'
    Package: rustc cargo libstd-rust-dev libstd-rust-*
    Pin: release n=trixie-backports
    Pin-Priority: 600
    PIN
    apt-get update && apt-get build-dep .
    # -> rustc, cargo 1.94.1+dfsg1-1~bpo13+3; debhelper stays at 13.24.2.

Installing the two directly works too:

    apt-get install -t trixie-backports rustc cargo

Why the floor is not lowered to meet trixie instead: 1.87 was chosen against
a security trade-off, not for convenience. Holding 1.85 would mean pinning
zbus and the whole family of crates around it, which would freeze the D-Bus
library of a *privileged* helper against its security updates. A build that
needs one extra apt line is the cheaper of the two.

The network
-----------

`debian/rules` calls the source tree's own `make build`, which expands to
`cargo build --release --locked`, and cargo resolves the dependencies in
Cargo.lock from crates.io. The build therefore needs network access, which
the Debian archive's own buildds do not grant. Building for the archive
rather than for yourself would need the crates vendored into the source
package (`cargo vendor` plus a `.cargo/config.toml` pointing at it), or the
dependencies packaged separately and built with dh-cargo. Neither is done
here.

The RPM has since grown the first of the two, which is the shape a Debian
equivalent would take: `%bcond_with vendor` and a second Source in
packaging/rpm/porthole.spec, and a %prep hunk that unpacks it and writes the
`replace-with = "vendored-sources"` stanza. Nothing is shared between the two
packagings, so it is an example rather than a head start.

CARGO_HOME is redirected to debian/cargo-home so the build does not write
into the build user's ~/.cargo; override_dh_auto_clean removes it.

The test suite
--------------

debian/rules overrides dh_auto_test with nothing in it. `cargo test
--workspace --exclude porthole-gui` passes on a developer machine.

The two reasons this section gave until 2026-09-10 have both stopped being
true, and debian/rules' own comment now carries the replacements. In short:
crates/porthole-cli/tests/cli.rs gives every porthole process it starts a
private bus of its own, so doctor_says_the_helper_is_missing_when_it_is no
longer needs the machine to have a system bus -- measured with both bus
variables pointing at nothing: 4 passed, 0 failed. And a release build no
longer fails the tests that need PORTHOLE_STATE_FILE, PORTHOLE_DEVICES_FILE
or the helper's --session, all three of which are still honoured only where
cfg!(debug_assertions) holds: those tests carry
#[cfg_attr(not(debug_assertions), ignore = ...)] and a release run reports
them as ignored, by name. Measured: 37 passed, 0 failed, 12 ignored.

What debian/control is missing is dbus-run-session and busctl, which the
suite now needs on PATH and which packaging/rpm/porthole.spec declares as
BuildRequires. Nothing has been measured in a debian:13 chroot since the two
old reasons stopped applying, so declaring them is where re-adding the run
would start, not where it would finish.

Build profiles
--------------

porthole-gui links GTK4 and libadwaita, and its build-dependencies
(libgtk-4-dev, libadwaita-1-dev) are behind the pkg.porthole.nogui build
profile. A host without those headers can still produce the porthole binary
package:

    DEB_BUILD_PROFILES=pkg.porthole.nogui apt-get build-dep .
    DEB_BUILD_PROFILES=pkg.porthole.nogui dpkg-buildpackage -b -us -uc

That drops the porthole-gui package entirely. It is the same line the source
tree's Makefile draws with WITH_GUI=0. Run on 2026-09-11 in the debian:13
container tests/container/Containerfile.deb-upgrade builds -- rustc and cargo
from trixie-backports, no libgtk-4-dev installed: two files out,
porthole_1.0.0-1_amd64.deb and porthole-dbgsym_1.0.0-1_amd64.deb.

lintian raises one tag on that pair, and it is left standing rather than
overridden:

    W: porthole: command-with-path-in-maintainer-script /usr/bin/porthole
       (in test syntax) [prerm:44]

which is the `[ -x /usr/bin/porthole ]` guard in debian/porthole.prerm. The
absolute path is the point there: the prerm has to find *this package's own*
binary, at the location this package installed it, while it is still on disk,
and a maintainer script running as root is not a place to let $PATH decide
which `porthole` gets executed. lintian's own exit status is 0 -- it is a
warning, not an error -- so the tag is recorded here rather than silenced in
debian/porthole.lintian-overrides, where a reader would have to go looking for
it to find out it had been raised at all.

The file list
-------------

There is none, on purpose. debian/rules calls `make install` and
`make install-gui`; the Makefile at the top of the tree is the only place the
install layout is written down, and crates/porthole-cli/tests/install_layout.rs
compares that layout for equality on every `cargo test`. A debian/*.install
file would be a second copy, free to drift from the one the test guards.

Where the helper goes
---------------------

/usr/libexec/porthole-helper, and nowhere else.
data/com.jacopobriccola.Porthole.service (the D-Bus activation file) and
data/porthole-helper.service (the systemd unit) both name that path
literally, and the bus and systemd read it out of the installed file.
`make install-helper` greps both and stops when LIBEXECDIR disagrees with
them, so debian/rules passes PREFIX=/usr and lets that check run.

Maintainer scripts
------------------

Almost all of them are generated:

  dh_installsystemd -pporthole --no-enable --no-start porthole-helper.service
      preinst/postinst/postrm snippets for the system unit.
  dh_installsystemduser -pporthole porthole-agent.service
      postinst/postrm snippets for the user unit, enabled globally so a
      graphical login starts it.
  dh_icons
      generates nothing here, on purpose: hicolor-icon-theme carries
      `interest-noawait /usr/share/icons/hicolor`, so dpkg's trigger does the
      cache update and dh_icons skips the hicolor and gnome themes.

Three more directories are covered by dpkg triggers rather than by anything
in this package:

  /usr/share/dbus-1/system.d and /usr/share/dbus-1/system-services
      interest-noawait in both dbus and dbus-broker.
  /usr/share/applications
      interest-noawait in desktop-file-utils.

/usr/share/metainfo has no such trigger -- appstream 1.0.5 registers
interest-noawait only on the three /usr/share/app-info directories -- and
nothing here adds one.

debian/porthole.prerm is the one hand-written script, and it exists because
dh_installsystemd emits its stop-on-remove `unless NO_START`: passing
--no-start to keep a D-Bus activated service from being started at install
time also drops the stop. Its own comment says the rest.

What removing the package does about open ports
-----------------------------------------------

It closes them. debian/porthole.prerm runs `timeout 60 /usr/bin/porthole
close --all` before the helper is stopped, and the helper cancels each
opening's transient expiry timer as it closes it -- so nothing is left
pointing at a /usr/bin/porthole the removal is about to delete. This
paragraph said the opposite until this commit; the close landed in 34eeebe
and the sentence stayed behind.

`remove` only. dpkg calls the same prerm for `upgrade`, `deconfigure` and
`failed-upgrade`, and closing every open port on a version bump would take
away access the user arranged as the price of it. `dpkg --purge` of an
installed package runs `prerm remove` first, so purge takes this branch too.

Never fatal, and that is what leaves cases still worth knowing about. The
call can fail with no system bus, with polkit not running, or against a
helper an earlier failed upgrade left broken; the removal then continues and
prints a warning naming `firewall-cmd --list-rich-rules`, `ufw status
numbered` and `nft list ruleset`. Three more removals reach no close at all,
because the guards in the prerm exclude them: DPKG_ROOT set (an install into
a chroot), no /run/systemd/system (a machine not running systemd, an image
build), and /usr/bin/porthole not executable. A reboot closes everything in
any of those cases, because porthole writes no permanent rule, and
`porthole close --all` before the removal closes it sooner.

packaging/rpm/porthole.spec's %preun and packaging/aur/porthole.install's
pre_remove are the same decision on the other two distributions.
